      "writtenOn": "2026-04-21T11:53:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In other words: so, we are just _afraid_ that in some cases the default reset mechanism (i.e. ACPI reset aka PCI warm reset) will not succeed for us for some reason, so we are implementing this fallback sequence, trying to partially mimic what the host kernel does; whereas we just _hope_ that this fallback sequence will actually succeed in that case, since we don\u0027t know how to test that (since we have no hardware on which we would actually observe any problems with the default reset mechanism).\n\nSo I\u0027m just not sure if all this makes sense to do.\n\nAnyway, if we still want to do this and thus want this pkvm_udelay(), the problem I see with it is: it looks like a generic API, which could be potentially reused by pKVM for other cases, if it needed to wait for a specified amount of time for any other reasons; whereas with this WARN_ON_ONCE(!tsc_khz) fallback we are assuming a specific use case, i.e. the emergency reset.\n\nSo if we really want to implement pkvm_udelay(), maybe we\u0027d better just _require_ non-zero tsc_khz, i.e. fail pKVM initialization if it zero? I think modern CPUs should always have a reliable TSC anyway?",
      "parentUuid": "e11e0cfa_4d242eb5",
      "revId": "8a9b85cbced8a0fad005b835c8a409a6b7fb1b96",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
